      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a18.6.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g18.6.6', 'g18.6.12', 'g18.4.3'],
          lemma: "Ἐγώ εἰμι … ἔπεσαν χαμαί",
          title: "“I am he” — and they fell to the ground",
          body: "John will not let us mistake the arrest for a scene of helplessness. Jesus is not seized in the dark by surprise; **“knowing all that would happen to him,”** he *comes forward* to meet the mob and asks the questions. And when he answers their search — *“Whom do you seek?”* / *“Jesus of Nazareth”* — with the two words **“I am”** (*egō eimi*), the whole armed cohort **draws back and falls to the ground.** John surely intends more than a stumble in the torchlight. *I AM* is the divine name (Exodus 3:14), the same self-declaration that runs like a golden thread through this Gospel; here it flashes out once with a hint of its glory, and soldiers with swords go down like men before the ark. The point is unmistakable: no one takes this life; he lays it down. The one being arrested is the one in command of the arrest. And in the same breath he shepherds his own to safety — *“if you seek me, let these men go”* (v. 8) — the Good Shepherd standing between the wolves and the flock even as he is taken. He goes to the cross not as a victim overpowered but as a King who has chosen the road.",
        },
      ],
