      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a18.36.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g18.36.4', 'g18.36.28', 'g18.36.42'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“My kingdom is not of this world”",
          body: "Pilate can conceive of only one kind of king — the kind Rome deals in, with soldiers and territory — so he asks, *“Are you the King of the Jews?”* Jesus’ answer redraws the whole map of power: **“My kingdom is not of this world.”** Notice carefully what he does *not* mean. He does not mean his kingdom is merely spiritual, or otherworldly, or has no claim on earth — it will one day fill the earth. He means his kingdom does not *originate from* this world and does not *operate by* its methods. The proof he gives is telling: **“if my kingdom were of this world, my servants would be fighting”** — but they are not; he has just told Peter to sheathe his sword. A kingdom that spread by the sword would be exactly the kind of kingdom he came to overthrow. His reign advances not by killing but by *dying,* not by seizing power but by laying down his life, not by making others suffer but by suffering for others. That is why the King stands *bound* before Pilate and does not resist: this is not weakness but the very shape of his rule. And it exposes the tragedy of the moment — the world’s greatest power (Rome) and the world’s holiest religion (the temple) have joined hands to condemn the one true King, and neither can recognize him, because he does not look like power to eyes trained on thrones. The kingdom of God comes not down from Caesar’s palace but up from a criminal’s cross.",
        },
      ],
