      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a18.38.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g18.38.7', 'g18.37.32', 'g18.38.24'],
          lemma: "Τί ἐστιν ἀλήθεια;",
          title: "“What is truth?”",
          body: "It is one of the most famous questions in history, and the most tragic — because the answer is standing bound before him. Jesus has just said he came *“to bear witness to the truth”* (v. 37); the man who is *the* Truth (14:6), the Word made flesh, is an arm’s length away. And Pilate replies, **“What is truth?”** — and does not wait for an answer, turning on his heel to go back out. It can be read as a sneer (*truth — a philosopher’s toy, and I have an execution to manage*), or as a weary shrug (*who can know?*), or as a genuine flicker of longing snuffed out by expedience. John does not tell us the tone, and perhaps all three are in it. What he does show is the awful irony: the one man who could have answered stands there, and Pilate walks away — and in walking away, hands over the innocent he has just declared innocent (*“I find no guilt in him”*) to be crucified. Here is worldly power at its most exposed: it can ask the great question and refuse the great Answer in the same breath, because the truth is *inconvenient* and the crowd is loud. Pilate becomes the patron of every age that treats truth as negotiable and then washes its hands of the consequences. The question hangs unanswered in the room — but the Answer will speak from a cross, and rise.",
        },
      ],
